We need to assert for all int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); (or maybe size() before call). Since we need old size? The method does not modify the queue, just returns copy. So size after call equals old size. But we can just use this.size() (post-state) which equals old size. However to be precise we could use \old(this.size()).

The property: if this.get(i) == null then \result[i] == null else this.get(i).equals(\result[i])? Since elements may be objects, equality may be via equals. The comment says "element in the queue at index i is equal to the element at index i in the returned array." Could use == or equals? Usually for objects they refer to equality (equals). Use .equals. But need to handle null case separately because calling equals on null would cause NPE. So we use conditional: (this.get(i) == null) => \result[i] == null && (this.get(i) != null) => this.get(i).equals(\result[i]).

We can combine with implication: (\old(this.get(i)) == null) => \result[i] == null && (\old(this.get(i)) != null) => \old(this.get(i)).equals(\result[i])? Since get(i) unchanged, we could use this.get(i). Use \old maybe not needed. Since method does not modify queue, old equals new. Use this.get(i) is fine.

Thus assert: \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); (this.get(i) == null) => \result[i] == null && (this.get(i) != null) => this.get(i).equals(\result[i]);

Need parentheses to group correctly. Use && between two implications.

Write:

